In article <2geofjINNmkp@matt.ksu.ksu.edu>, rrsum@matt.ksu.ksu.edu (Rick Summerhill) writes:
> 1) Do Universities that have dorms on the network allow free access to
> the entire Internet, or do they only allow access to the local campus
> network.  Access to only the campus network essentially would require
> students to login to a campus machine, and thereby presenting a
> passwd, to get access to the entire network!  Are there other solutions?
> Do folks use Kerberos?
> 
Most of our Internet access, including from dorms, is through our VAX
Cluster.  Anyone with a username, which means any faculty/staff member
or student, can get a VAX account at no charge.  All VAX users sign an
agreement that covers some ethics questions, but beyond that we do not 
police the use of Internet.  We do investigate and take whatever
action is warranted when cases of abuse or harassment arise.

> 2) What do universities do about security in dorms, given the fact
> that there are freely available packages that run on PCs and can watch
> packets!  Do folks use Kerberos again?
> 
At present we have mostly ISN in our dorms, and so the security
problems are minimal.  We are installing ethernet as the dorms are
remodelled, and we have not yet solved the security problem.  We are
not using Kerberos.  Very few of our desktop stations could run it.

> I would be very interested in hearing about policies like this!  I
> will gladly summarize the results and post them to the same groups to
> which this query is being posted.

