From caf-talk Caf Jan 18 08:24:08 1994
From: nicka@mccmedia.com (Nick Arnett)
Subject:  Re: New lawsuit: Is CompuServe liable for infringing uploads?
Message-ID: <nicka-160194140652@mccmedia.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Jan 1994 22:17:47 GMT

In article <2745@beacon.rain.com>, Steven.Horn@f1.n3409.z1.fidonet.org
(Steven Horn) wrote:

> Surely Compuserve is aware of the difficulties created by allowing users to
> upload files into a common area and allowing others to download immediately? 
> We are are all aware of cases where BBSes have been shut down for making
> commercial files available and no prudent BBS operator would allow a user to
> download a file which had not been vetted first.

You have to make a distinction between CompuServe and the companies or
individuals who operate forums under contract to it.  My company operates
the Multimedia Forums on CompuServe, which means that there are
non-disclosures (and pragmatic considerations) in the way of my discussing
this issue too openly, but...

No upload to our forum becomes available to the public without being
reviewed, but the reviews are done by volunteers in exchange for free forum
access.  We certainly train the volunteers and look over their shoulders,
but the system is far from perfect.

We don't have much trouble training people to scan for viruses and
*unambiguous* copyright infringements -- pirated software, documents
created by someone other than the uploader that don't bear any kind of
redistribution permission -- but there are all sorts of ambiguous possible
copyright infringements uploaded.  There are endless audio clips, bits of
code, etc. that might appear.  The task of identifying each possible
infringement is hopeless.

The reaction of some sysops is to reject almost everything, only allowing
original works by the uploader.  That greatly diminishes the value of the
forums, IMO.

My reaction is to set up forum policies that make it clear to the users
that it is a public forum for them to exchange files and that they, the
uploaders, are responsible for liability for copyright, etc.  But to have
any chance of that standing up in court, we also exercise as little
judgement as possible when it comes to the value of any uploaded file.  We
are contracturally obligated to enforce CompuServe's rules, so we do.  But
we try to go no further, in hopes that a court would view us like any other
distributor of information, not liable for the actions of our users. 
Otherwise, we either have to severely limit the usefulness of the forum or
raises prices considerably to beef up our screening process and pay for
liability insurance.  I would hope that through the efforts of EFF and
others, we won't even have to worry about the latter scenario.

I don't think that we should bear zero liability, like a common carrier,
but I think our liability should be limited to gross negligence, such as
knowingly allowing a commercial, copyrighted software program to be
distributed via our forums, or deliberate infringements.

Of course, it all gets a bit twisted when you realize that everyone
involved as an operator of the forum is also a *user* of the forum, but I
would hope that a court would treat me differently in each role.  When I
upload a file myself, I make sure that there's nothing infringing in it.

