In article <2hhrs5$3ne@ghost.mcrcim.mcgill.edu>,
David Jones <djones@CIM.McGill.CA> wrote:
[delete summary of and preamble to policy]
>WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?
>    1) You are allowed to use the computer systems to aid in the
>    completion of your courses.
>    2) You are allowed to use mail to correspond with associates 
>    around the university and around the world.
>    3) You are allowed to use the resources of the computer system to
>    further your academic pursuits.
>    4) You are not allowed to talk, telnet, or ftp to computers not on
>    Scarborough Campus. You are not allowed to possess pornography in
>    any form, files which are unacademic, files which are to used for
>    subverting the system, files which may be deemed offensive by
>    any one person, or one group. Needless to say, you are not 
>	allowed to use anyone else's account (anywhere in the world)!
>    5) If you have any files which are "unacademic" you must IMMEDIATELY
>    remove them from the University's computer systems.
>
I would say that further goals number (1) and (4) could arise in conflict
very easily. It would be very difficult to write anything opinion oriented
about, say the Holocaust, immigration or other potentially controversial
issue, without offending *some* group. This policy would allow the
deletion of the file if "any one person" was offended. Who is going to
judge what stays and what goes? 

I guess this means that Philip Rushton couldn't be a guest lecturer at
Scarborough and put his lecture notes on the system. Indeed, it could be
argued that someone writing a critique refuting Rushton's work would not
be allowed to keep an electronic copy of Rushton's work on the account.
The more I think about (4) the bigger the potential trouble it seems that
the university has created for itself. I think I see what they are after
... i.e. reasoned and balanced use of limited resources ... I just believe
a better worded policy would satisfy most of these concerns. 

Darren Meister

P.S. I agree that not allowing gopher/news access to do academic searches
is a real dent in academic productivity. Every undergrad/grad/faculty
should be shown the potential resources that exist in order to facilitate
better and more expedient literature review and data collection.  


