Oh, you presume?  I presume that a professor can arrange for external access
if the coursework requires it, but what about those who _want_ to use the
powerful tools, but are capriciously denied access?  I've never been instructed
to use gopher or ftp in a class, yet they I use them routinely. 

]>>And, BTW, "offensive files" aren't going to be offending anyone except
]>>spies snooping through other people's accounts.

]It sounds draconian, but
]people are granted access to these machines for coursework, not
]entertainment. 

Entertainment | coursework is not a neatly drawn line.  I've accessed loads
of files for "entertainment" purposes that later significantly contributed
to papers written for classes.  Is U Toronto a place of learning, or a
conditioning facility that indoctrinates students with only University-
supplied knowledge?  Some facilities call irc and Usenet "games," yet I've
learned more directly-applicable information from sci.electronics than from
ECE classes, so far.  There are many "serious" irc channels used for academic
purposes.  Learning is more than sitting in a lecture and reading a textbook.

]The University can't afford the legal fees that would be
]required to defend itself from a suit brought about as the result of
]inappropriate use of its computers. 

Cover-your-ass policies can backfire when people sue because the strict
controls weren't strict enough.  Generally, adherence to a philosophy of
academic freedom offers greater indemnity.  Canadian laws may vary, though.

