If an article in a newsgroup a site carries is cross-posted to a group the
site doesn't carry, the news software creates a directory for the
non-carried group and links the file (article) to that directory.

Well, that was as clear as mud, wasn't it?  Let me try an example:

I carry talk.politics.drugs here at pofbbs, but I don't carry alt.drugs.  A
fair number of articles are cross-posted between these groups.  When an
article comes in for t.p.d that is also cross-posted to a.d, it is stored
in t.p.d under a filename that is the next available article number for
t.p.d.  Then it is 'linked' to a.d under the next available article number
there.  It is still only one file, but it can be referred to by two names:
'/usr/spool/news/talk/politics/drugs/1234' and
'/usr/spool/news/alt/drugs/321'.  (Those article numbers are made up, of
course.)

IF a user has "alt.drugs" in his .newsrc, it will look to him or her like a
few articles a day are coming into a.d.

There is no way around this.  This is how the software keeps track of
things.

So, I'm willing to bet that your site is NOT expending energy deleting the
binaries from a.b.e.  It just doesn't get them.  The reason you see any
articles at all in a.b.e is because some articles in non-binary groups are
cross-posted there, and the news software keeps track of this fact by
sticking a reference to the article in the otherwise inactive group called
a.b.e.  Therefore, all you see is non-binaries in that group.

In article <CJDM3p.HAw@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
>It *might* have a legitimate reason for not carrying the binaries. For

Uh, "*might* have a legitimate reason for not carrying" a group, Carl?  Are
you suggesting that "I don't want to carry that group" isn't a
more-than-good-enough reason?

(Sorry, that wasn't meant to be as harsh as it sounds.  Just asking,
really, not attacking or flaming.)

>But even if it does have a legitmate reason for expunging some
>articles, it should rename the result so that readers are not mislead
>into thinking that are reading unfiltered netnews.

Well, as I said, they are almost certainly not expending computer and
communications resources to receive a.b.e only to expend more resources
deleting stuff.

Your suggested solution is reasonable and desireable but, alas, not
possible.  What *could* be done would be to explicitly 'expire' a.b.e as
quickly as possible.  The articles would still show up there for some amount
of time, though.  For example, if the expiry process is run once every day,
then the articles would appear there for up to one day.

The real solution is to not have a.b.e in the user's '.newsrc' file.  It's
possible and even likely that when Bert got his account, a.b.e wasn't in
the .newsrc he was supplied, since the group doesn't exist at his site.  He
may have added it in himself, and now he's seeing the oddities of how the
news system does its accounting and mistaking it for someone purposely
deleting all the goodies from a.b.e.

