Things at McGill aren't quite as draconian as Dr. Jones implies.
 
Allow me to first summarize part of the posting that was made to
the mcgill.general group.  I'll also add some comments of my own.
 
- Our news partition is filling up.  We felt that it was better
  to focus on groups that were of interest instead of simply
  reducing retention periods (again).  Clearly we will need to
  support the growing news volume in the long term, but it is
  not completely obvious that we can let it grow without some
  control.
 
- We currently carry 4400 groups.  Until now, we have automatically
  created all new groups that appeared on our doorstep.  As a
  result, although we have been running a news server for less time
  than many other locations, we carry more groups than many places.
  The sheer number has also caused problems with some news readers.
  Hopefully this will become less of a problem as people move
  to better news readers.
 
- The proposal was to remove hierarchies and groups that were not
  of interest.  The posting identified the hierarchies that we were
  questioning.  Many were geographic groups that we felt might not
  be actively used at McGill.  We do not currently carry all known
  geographic/organizational hierarchies (there are 215 of them
  in a recent list) and rarely are asked to carry those we do not
  currently have.  Similarly, because of how our server was
  historically configured, we have not carried all groups within
  some of these more obscure hierarchies, and we have had no
  complaints.  It seemed likely that some of those that we
  accidently carry may be of similarly low interest to our
  community.
 
- We made it clear that we have not decided on the exact method to
  determine which groups to carry, and we asked for suggestions and
  comments.  The lists that David Lawrence distributes could be
  a prime source of information.  Creating all groups and then
  deleting once that are not accessed much is another option.
 
- The original note to system administrators said that we wanted to
  start the process on the 24th.  Based on initial feedback, we
  delayed it by an additional two weeks to allow for plenty of comments.
 
The issue of whether to have a formal policy or just internal
procedures, is slated for discussion at an upcoming Senate Committee
on Computing (SCC), the University's formal body advising on computing
and networking matters.  The SCC includes representation from all
faculties, Senate and the McGill Libraries.  At the same time, we will
discuss who should be involved in the decision on whether to carry new
groups.  If, based on feedback, it appears that the inital triage to
reduce the number of groups is controversial, we will defer
implementation and discuss this triage at the SCC meeting as well (and
reduce the current retention periods to ensure that we do not run out
of space).
 
In talking to the system manager in Dr. Jones' department, we realized
that had not received the posting on which much of my comments
are based.  It is unfortunate that he posted here prior to raising
the issue in this forum.
 
