     If someone receives by email a truly confidential message, he or
she should promptly remove it from the system on which it was received.
Network-connected machines, especially ones shared with a large number
of other users who may not be terribly security conscious, are not good
places to keep confidential information.  Furthermore, whether or not
the policy says anything about it, the superuser on a system is always
able to read files left lying around.  You either have to trust this
person (not necessarily a good idea, unless you know him or her) or you
have to protect yourself by removing sensitive information from their
purview.  At least this policy makes that much clear to the students.
     As for the question of due process, I admit that I am not entirely
happy about the lack of it in the policy.  One assumes that a student
who loses his or her account could seek help from the University's
Ombudsman.  It's not an ideal arrangement, but it is a mechanism whereby
the case could be audited, albeit after the fact.
