In article <CJwBt6.L05@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:

While I do agree that the language of the policy is more than a little
over-harsh, I believe that it's worth making a few comments here.

>>    All students with accounts on these
>>    machines have been granted them solely for the purpose of furthering
>>    their academic pursuits. The University owns the computer systems, the
>>    accounts, the files, and the network.

>By definition, if the Univeristy owns the files, then it can sell
>them. Is this really that the University means to claim?

Absolutely.  It is part of the IP policy in place at the University of
Toronto that has been around for many years - well predating the
"computer age".  And is part of the University student rules and
regulations.  This is just a statement reflecting that policy as applied
to University-owned computing facilities.  And it's no different than
the IP policies of many corporations where they claim ownership of
anything invented using corporate resources.  Many corporations go
considerably farther, and claim everything "invented" during your
employment, regardless of whether corporate resources were used.  But
those overbroad policies get struck down when court challenged.

>Claiming
>ownership of someone else's intellectual material is a violation of
>the moral rights of the material's creator. It is called plagiarism.

These rules are part of the rules and regulations that the student
accepts when entering that university.  Thus, there's already a
contractual acceptance of such policies, and since those files were
made possible at the taxpayers expense, at least some moral right as well.

>> The University reserves the right to
>>    audit any and all files on the system.

>So any computer operator can (on his or her own) look at (for example)
>confidential on-line records of experiments on human subjects. If this
>is true, the Univeristy should lose its accreditation.

It's an enormous leap to assume that "The University ..." means "any computer
operator can (on his or her own)".  The statement is only providing fair
warning that files can be looked at.  As, for example, CERT has advised
installations to warn their users to attempt to circumvent legal
wrangles later if an investigation becomes necessary - remembering of
course, that US sites's snooping is still bound by the ECPA.

Whether an operator can snoop their own is something we cannot tell.
Many organizations will make statements such as this, but actually
looking will require "just cause" (ie: evidence arrived at through some
other means) and upper-level management approval.  Because they know
damn well that a court can rule against them if they don't do it right.
As such, this policy is nothing more than a simplified synopsis of
how the University rules and regulations apply - which do include such
things as "due process" etc.  The "letter of the (University) law" is
the student rules and regulations, appeal process, etc.  Something
drafted by the Scarborough campus with lax language like this is not
intended to be the "law".  The student rules and regulations are, and
are what apply if an actual incident arises.

Secondly, this is obviously tempered by constraints of what the law
allows the University to do.  If, for example, an operator, goes into
personal health records like this, there's any number of laws that can
end up putting the operator in prison or subject to heavy fines.  And if
the senate pulled such a stunt, not only could they lose their
accreditation, worse, they could lose their funding.  No matter what the
University seems to think they can do, they are still subject to the
law.  Thus, the policy may well overstate the University's rights, what
matters is what the student rules and regulations require, and University
actually tries to do.  A US site may do the same, but they're still subject
to the constraints of the ECPA.

>> Any files which are deemed
>>    "unacademic" in nature may and will be immediately purged.

>So the University has now given computer operators authority to decide
>on their own what is "academic" and what is not.

I don't see anything that asserts that a computer operator has that
authority.

>>    4) You are not allowed to talk, telnet, or ftp to computers not on
>>    Scarborough Campus. You are not allowed to possess pornography in
>>    any form, files which are unacademic, files which are to used for
>>    subverting the system, files which may be deemed offensive by
>>    any one person, or one group. Needless to say, you are not 
>>	allowed to use anyone else's account (anywhere in the world)!
>[...]

>The policy is deemed offensive by me. I am "any one person". I expect
>all the files that contain this policy to be "immediately purged" and
>the policy's creator to punished.

The language there is more than a little excessive, but it is consistent
with a fair warning that a policy is in place that says that material
used to harrass groups or individuals contrary to the law is subject to
removal.
