In article <CJvMsE.325@gpu.utcc.utoronto.ca> gregh@gpu.utcc.utoronto.ca (Greg Hurtault) writes:
>
> It's one thing to say that the UofT doesn't have unlimited resources,
> but it's quite another thing to say that they 'own' the files that
> YOU, the user may be creating... I find that implication quite
> offensive..  if I am the one who makes the file, regardless of where
> the files may reside, the files are MY property unless I say otherwise.

Nonsense.  If I draw a diagram on a blackboard in a classroom, do I then
own the blackboard?  Of course not; I only own (ie. retain copyright of)
the diagram.  I have no right to expect that the diagram will still be
there tomorrow, or even that I will be allowed to use the blackboard in
the future, because it doesn't belong to me.

Conversely, just because the University owns your files does not mean that
you have no rights with respect to their contents.  The files are not
themselves intellectual property: they are an expression of intellectual
property.  That means that even though the University owns the files,
they can't copy your term paper out of your account and publish it without
your permission.  (The Berne Convention supercedes University policy.)

> What if the files I have are 'secret' with regards to a project that
> I am working on? Does that give the UofT the right to 'peep' at them?
> I would think not.

Why would anyone in their right mind keep secret files on someone else's
computer?

    "Can I borrow your notebook?"
    "Sure."
    <next week:>
    "Here's your notebook.  Hey, don't read it!  I wrote some secrets in
     there! I don't want you to know them!"

And, yes, I'd say the UofT has the right to examine data stored on their
own disk drives.  Sort of implicit in the function of a disk drive, and
the meaning of the word "own."

If you want to keep private files and use gopher and ftp, then buy a
computer and disk drive and pay for an Internet connection, fer chrissake.

Incidentally, the policy statement posted in article
<2hhrs5$3ne@ghost.McRCIM.McGill.EDU> does not bear any signature; who
authored it, and where is it implied that it is official policy?  It
seems rather informally-worded for an official policy document.

