From caf-talk Caf Jan 21 04:23:02 1994
From: tboychuk@unixg.ubc.ca (Tim Boychuk)
Newsgroups: ont.general,tor.general,alt.privacy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Re: Cost of Netaccess at U Toronto
Date: 21 Jan 94 09:03:13 GMT
Message-ID: <tboychuk.759142993@unixg.ubc.ca>

mroussel@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca (Marc Roussel) writes:
>     You left out a few items:  The cost of the computers and terminals,
>including maintenance and administration, the cost of their side of the
>ethernet, including maintenance, ...  Maybe the lines out of the campus
>aren't a major item on a per capita basis, but the system that hangs off
>those lines is expensive.

It seems to me that there are two obvious solutions to the objections 
raised in this discussion and the one previous:

(1)  Open up the accounts in a UNIX shell and don't allow access to TELNET,
     ftp, Netnews, etc (ie, a menu system). 

Students, then, don't have to read the policy because access was really not 
available to them in the first place. I mean, the accounts have been granted
because the professor holding the course thought it would beneficial to use
some tools on the system available.

When I was an undergrad at University College and didn't have access to Unix
(nor had any idea of what it was), I was perfectly happy to go to Laidlaw
and use the undergrad terminals there to do their research. So, rather than
stating to the students: this is what the computer can do for you, but you 
can't use this, this, and that, say: here's what the menu will let you do.
End of story.

(2)  Make the students pay to use the extended services, which, therefore,
     will increase funding to the Scarborough College Computing Services, abd
     therefore will offset any money required to increase disk resources,
     etcetera.

So, if students want to download megabytes from A.B.P.E., let them! But make
them pay for the extended resources. It's clear that Universities in Ontario
are suffering from a funding crisis... if students want to get the extras,
then they should pay for it. 

Unfortunately, folks, Canadian Universities do have limits onto what they
can offer their students, both undergraduates and graduate. The limits can
range from underfunding of the Canada Student Loan program, to a lack of 
laboratory equipment, to disk space on a hard drive. If Scarborough College
wants to place limits on the users account, it's up to them. After all, they
are letting them have an account in the first place. It they want more, they
should pay for the services.

But once again, I think it's better to relax the limits and make the students
responsible for their own accounts rather than to have the *OVERSEER* System
Administrator try to maintain law and order. After all, the meaning of the
words "pornographic", and "unacademic", are cloudy at best, and are subject
to many different interpretations by many different people. If there is a 
concern over disk space and limits, then why let the students have the ac-
counts in the first place?

-Timothy A. Boychuk

