In article <wb8fozCJyo5I.370@netcom.com> wb8foz@skybridge.scl.cwru.edu
(David Lesher) writes:
>mroussel@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca (Marc Roussel) writes:
>>     Note that no user is allowed unlimited run of the libraries.  Some
>>journals aren't allowed to circulate outside the library.  All books
>>have return dates.  
>But they CAN look at books in the library, correct? Or do they
>need special permission from a prof to look at each and every
>one? After all, it consumes resources to have to reshelve that
>book afterwards.
>
>>Since they have email they have
>>access to a wide variety of services (including ftp).  They just don't
>>get the instant gratification of interactive network services.
>
>"Since they are free to go to public libraries off campus and
>request inter-library loans, they have access to a wide variety
>of services. They just do not have the 'instant gratification' of
>browsing in OUR library, and learning something in the process."

     I was a little reticent to bring up the library analogy because I
don't think the two can be compared all that directly.  All I was trying
to point out is that there are limitations to the use of any campus
service.  Direct comparison of these limitations is largely pointless
because the services themselves are not comparable.  For one thing (and
I think this is a crucial point) undergraduate access to
network-connected computers is currently limited (certainly on St-George
campus, probably also at Scarborough) to people in programs like computer
science and engineering.  There are a few PC and Mac labs around
for everyone else's wordprocessing and some departments (like physics)
have computer labs of their own.  The purpose of the workstation
clusters is therefore clearly very limited.  On the other hand, the
libraries are a shared community resource.  Everyone uses the same
libraries, from the undergrads to the faculty.  That puts library
services in a very different category than computer services.
Nevertheless, if you really want to explore the analogy further,
consider this:  University libraries try to comprehensively collect only
in areas of academic study.  They don't usually buy every dime novel
published even though these might be enormously popular among students
seeking a little diversion.  I think that this is comparable to asking
students to limit their use of the machines to clearly identifiable
academic work.
     One last comment:  David wrote

>After all, it consumes resources to have to reshelve that
>book afterwards.

Librarians like to reshelve books themselves, but it is becoming
increasingly common for them to ask users to reshelve journals whenever
possible.  It seems that budgetary restrictions are hitting everyone and
we all just have to cope as best we can.

				Marc R. Roussel
                                mroussel@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca

