     Don't use the computer to do that.  If the sysadmin is an idiot,
you should know better than this.  For that matter, if the sysadmin is
an idiot, it doesn't matter what the official policy is since he or she
can always peek around using his or her superuser priviledges.

>	What if the confidential message arrives and sits in the mailbox
>	and is read by the sysadmin before the student even sees it?

     You think that they have the time to look through all the
mailboxes?  Most of these systems have hundreds of accounts.  Most of
the mail would be "Hi, how are you?" messages.  You're either
extraordinarily paranoid or you're playing devil's advocate.

>>Furthermore, whether or not
>>the policy says anything about it, the superuser on a system is always
>>able to read files left lying around.
>
>	This is plain stupid.  The Chair of the dept has keys to
>	everyone's offices and has easy access to everyone's incoming
>	paper mail.  Are you saying that Professors should "expect"
>	these abilities to be abused?

     No.  On the other hand, most faculty have locked desks and/or
cabinets.  They don't leave confidential material lying on their desks
because, in addition to the Chair, many people have keys (caretakers,
etc.).  Besides, the Chair would have a lot to lose if he was caught
entering faculty offices without due cause.
     I should restate my point.  Unix system security is such that it is
imprudent to leave any really confidential, personal information on
them.  Sysadmins don't generally make a practice of looking at user
files unless they suspect a problem.  On the other hand, if you don't
know your sysadmin, and since Unix systems don't maintain significant
audit trails, you shouldn't rely on that.

>>re: due process.  One assumes that a student who loses his or her account
>> could seek help from the University's Ombudsman.
>
>	"One assumes" ?

