Tim Boychuk <tboychuk@unixg.ubc.ca> wrote:
>Suggestion: Open up the Scarborough Campus computers. Give them "nn" dollars
>to play with. Set up a charging system, and charge students for going over
>the limit. 

I used to work with a few systems under such schemes.  All I can say is "Feh."

>Suggestion (2):  Have the University open up an account where all students
>can have access, if they want, by paying for it.
>
>[...]
>
>So, inotherwords, make the students responsible for themselves rather than
>have an ominous overseer seemingly watching everything they do.

This opens up a *massive* can of worms.  For instance, do you charge a 
flat fee for diskspace, or does the price escalate with each user's con-
sumption?  Should 9600 baud connections cost more than 1200 baud connec-
tions, since the 9600 baud user will actually get more work accomplished?

This can really turn into a "haves and have-nots" situation.  Should the
students with money get better grades on projects, since he can afford to
use all these neat tools that the student beside him cannot use?

I'd rather see a consistent set of regulations/restrictions than a system
that favors kids with money.  If there are real problems with resources, 
I would consider a time/user restriction, similar to those placed on games
at many sites.  Something like "IRC and FTP access for general users will
only be enabled between 10 pm and 6 am,"  "Only 6 concurrent FTP sessions
will be allowed at any one time," or "Off-campus email messages may be 
queued for up to 6 hours before delivery"  would seem to treat everyone 
fairly.

I'd like to see UT-Scarborough try some restrictions along these lines
before removing access completely.

--Wes

