|>     imposed on all. 

No flames, but a couple of observations and questions drawn from the life, so to
speak.

The whole question is arising (again?) of what actually is "private" or belongs
to the individual user on a public or multiuser system that someone else pays
for.  I don't know if this issue has been resolved in the courts (US or Canadian
will do).  But it does introduce the question of an organization's right to
determine what it shall look at in a user's files, the pretexts it might use to
do so, and the actions it takes based on what it finds.

Okay, I'm weaseling.  IT doesn't find anything.  SomeONE normally--in my
experience--in a line organization asks the computer center people to
investigate a particular user's usage and to read--or at least print out--the
"offender's" files.  The job gets assigned to a systems administrator.

In 1985 I was fired from a job at Bellcore after my department management
decided to find pretexts for contractor-dumping (I learned this through a
contact in my Division head's office who was feeding her boyfriend information;
he in turn was passing it to me.)  In my case they found that I'd been reading
Usenet (then Netnews) on company time, even though the facility came
pre-installed on the machine to which I was assigned.  They printed out my
files, including stored mail and my resume; and were particularly incensed that
I'd posted my resume to misc.jobs.resumes because that "made Bellcore look bad."

I'm still living that one down.

In 1987, when I was working at AT&T Network Systems in Piscataway, NJ, I sent a
piece of email one morning to a friend in another building: in fact the same
boyfriend of the woman who was sneaking information out of the Bellcore Division
head's office two years before.  His mail was its usual collection of
obscenities and I answered in kind.  This was private mail, right?  Wrong. 
Twenty minutes later I passed a system administrator in the hall and she said
"Hey Ken, you put some pretty salty language in your mail, don't you?"  I
freaked.  "Did you read my mail?"  "We've been ordered to read all the outgoing
mail so nobody leaks proprietary information."  Right.  AT&T has cornered the
market on Shit and Fuck....watch their next commercials...or their next garbage
product.

The author of the original posting may be too busy keeping his system from
blowing up to care about what's in someone's private mail, but I'm suggesting
that not all SAs are similarly busy, and that some of them can be employed to
use a position where they have great power (over my files) but no authority (to
tell their boss to go fuck himself) precisely to monitor and oversee what people
write; and to allow those employers to propagate the myths of "company time" and
"productivity" or "academics" to deal with people who might have become
inconvenient for any number of reasons.

Now, was it flames we were discussing?....
