> >>  As for copyright, what is that
> >>worth when we're talking about undergraduate computing assignments?
> >>They are not original
> 
>      How often does that happen while working on assignments?  How original
> can you be while solving the Towers of Hanoi problem or being the
> zillionth undergrad to implement file locking in an OS course?
> 
> 
>      If the University hadn't given you access to computing resources,
> site-licensed software, and so on, could you have created this work?
> If you profited from your work, would you voluntarily reimburse the
> University for its expenses?  Intellectual property isn't created in a
> vacuum.


Section 13(3) of the Copyright Act, R.S.C. 1985, c. C-42, as amended 
states the following:

"Where the authour of a work was in the employment of some other person 
under a contract of service or apprenticeship and the work was made in 
the course of his employment by that person, the person by whom the 
author was employed shall, in the absence of any agreement to the 
contrary, be the first owner of the copyright....."

This says nothing about the university owning work produced by a student. 
s.13(1) of the ACT says:

"Subject to the Act, the author of a work shall be the first owner of the 
copyright therein."

and s.13(4) indicates:

"The owner of the copyright in any work may assign the right ... but no 
assignment or grant is valid unless it is in writing signed by the owner 
of the right in respect of which the assignment or grant is made or by 
his duly authorized agent."

BTW, a student who wrote a computer program to solve a very common 
problem and who came up with NO new ideas in solving the problem would 
still own copyright in that program as long as s/he did not copy the 
program from some other source.

