     I only have so much time for Usenet.  As the late Larry Lippman used to
say, "Usenet isn't life."  Sometimes, I decide not to respond.
I consider it a little rude and presumptuous on your part to expect
otherwise, but if you insist, I'll respond to these comments.  Don't
expect that I'll do it again however.
     If sanctions are applied against a student which make it impossible
for him or her to complete a course, the University's student
disciplinary process becomes involved.  It's up to a duly constituted
tribunal to decide how to handle these matters.  The sysadmins have a
certain amount of power, but in the long run they can't do much more
than make pests of themselves.  Whether or not they should have that
much power is another issue altogether.
     I'm very concerned about privacy issues, but I don't feel that
these particular machines, given the uses they are supposed to be put
to, are a good test case for such matters.  We have no constitutional
right to privacy in Canada.  There is a certain amount of common law and
specific legislation that gives us a minimal level of privacy, but
most of it is stated in terms of property rights (also not
constitutionally based, by the way), with obvious implications for
files on machines owned by someone else.  As a result, it's up to
each of us to take measures to protect ourselves from intrusion.
Since you have no legal protection, you shouldn't assume (even if no
one tells you they'll be looking) that email is a private channel.
It does absolutely no good to give people a false sense of security
by pretending that it is.  I use real mail for all truly private
communication.  There are some hazards, but at least it has the
protection of law.

