In article <1994Jan22.022221.2823@utopia.druid.com> djast@utopia.druid.com
(Dan Astoorian) writes:
>You don't give undergrads much credit, do you?  I know a fellow who,
>while he was still an undergraduate at the University of Waterloo,
>created an improvement to (if memory serves) the Boyer-Moore string
>searching algorithm.  That algorithm is taught in undergraduate classes
>and could easily be the topic of an assigment.
>
>The improvement he made was subsequently published in an ACM journal--
>without any mention of the undergrad's name, as far as I know.  This sort
>of thing should never happen at any respectable institution.  As it happens,
>it didn't; at the time he was on a co-op work term, at Microsoft.

...and Microsoft owned his work.
     However, I'll admit that my original posting was poorly stated.
Universities typically lay claim to any invention done with their
equipment.  Copyright is a different matter.  Some of my postings have
confused the two.
     To clarify my position, as my as my understanding of the
University's, let's analyze a few examples.  At best, an algorithm can be
patented.  Suppose that this undergraduate's algorithm was patentable.
Since (I'm guessing) it's a purely conceptual advance that did not
require use of University facilities for its development, the student
would probably have complete control of this invention.  On the other
hand, suppose that the student's invention was a piece of hardware built
with University parts.  The University contributed materially to this
invention and thus has a perfectly legitimate claim to part of it.
     What about copyright?  Wouldn't a student (or faculty member) use
the libraries (a University resource) in developing the work?  Yes, but
there is a crucial distinction:  The University owns the books but not
the ideas in them.  The ideas are the only important part of the book so
the University's contribution is incidental.  Where does that leave
software?  I'm not sure.  It seems to fall somewhere in between.  A lot
of the University's software base isn't its own.  It is licensed, in
one way or other.  On the other hand, the University has clear ownership
of the machines.  Since software development isn't possible without a
computer, the issue is much trickier IF THE UNIVERSITY OWNS THE COMPUTER.
(Universities typically only own a fraction of the computers on their
campuses.  The rest are usually bought with grant money and are "owned"
by the grantee.)

				Marc R. Roussel
                                mroussel@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca

