Well, if it's that easy, and that inexpensive, then why don't you just
get a couple thousand students to contribute to a machine separate from
the University's?  You can probably get a really nice machine and
operating system for only about five dollars per person.  Since disk
space is so cheap, they could chip in for as much space as they want for
their GIFs and computer games.  Oh, don't forget to get serial ports and
phone lines so that people can connect to it.  That'll be cheaper than
getting a whole lab of terminals.  You could probably get a dozen serial
ports/network cards for just a few dollars per person, and the phone
lines will only be pennies per month per person.  Oh, and of course
you'll need to rent a physical location for the machine to occupy.  Drop
in the bucket with that many people.  And, of course, you'll want to
connect to the Internet so that the students can use gopher, ftp, and
all those services which UofT won't provide for free; you probably can
get that for under a dollar per person.  And the cost of hiring people
to make sure the machine stays up, and for people to call when they have
computing problems, and to look after administrative duties such as
security (since you wouldn't want any hackers getting in and messing up
this nice setup) would only be about a penny an hour!  I'm sure that
maintenance (or maintenance contracts) won't be too much extra money
either.

