In article <1994Jan22.192706.21112@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca>, mroussel@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca (Marc Roussel) writes:

[ ... lots of quite sensible stuff deleted ...]

|> (Universities typically only own a fraction of the computers on their
|> campuses.  The rest are usually bought with grant money and are "owned"
|> by the grantee.)
|> 

Nope.  Computers bought with grant money ARE University property. They
are controlled by the grant holders, but not owned. The degree of control,
in practice, is considerably greater than NSERC, for example, has envisaged
in its regulations. If the University wanted greater administrative
control, it would certainly be entitled to it. It is only established
custom and the need for a flexible and informal research environment
which at U of T has allowed numerous independent computing "fiefdoms" 
to develop. The regime we now have is extremely liberal, but the
resources are limited. Don't forget, grant-funded computers are
intended for research, not undergraduate teaching. Teaching resources
at U of T are definitely threadbare, despite excellent work by many
people. For the shoestring budgets involved, very good facilities
have been developed, but are unable to accomodate anything but the
most basic needs. 

The articles by Tim Boychuk of UBC have, I think, offered the most
sensible counterpoint to the Scarborough position. I spent 6 
years at UBC, and I've been 12 years at U of T. Ever since the mid-1960's,
UBC has placed much higher priority on providing computing services to
all parts of the University than any other institution I've spent time
at, Caltech among others. UBC actually ENCOURAGES people to try new
applications (for example, we were formatting theses by computer
in the mid-1970's, with computer resources donated by the computer centre).
It sounds as though UBC is doing the same now with gopher, WWW etc.
This takes an agressive investment in support personnel, software
and equipment; the "corporate culture" making this a high priority
just does not exist at most other universities. U of T, for example, is
fragmented into 3 campuses (and some other sites, such as mine ...), 
many colleges, Faculties and Departments. Even within small departments
there are several independent computing "fiefdoms" based on research
groups, often with little cooperation and lots of duplication. This
fragmentation is a result of both the fragmented structure of U of T
and the fact that the old UTCS never had the resources and daring
of  UBC's computer centre. On the other hand, the present UTCC does a
fine job of wide-area networking.

