to your posting, I am performing a task that is unacademic and I should be 
banned from my university computing facility. 
 
Post-secondary education is subsidized, why? Perhaps to make it more 
accessible to the public? Although that you may not agree me, but I 
believe that by making education more accessible, the society benefits. 
Similar arguments applies to these 'extended services'. By levying a 
fee, students are discouraged to use these services, and that is a very 
unfortunate circumstance. Perhaps you have noticed as well that there is 
a increase in frequency that Internet is being mentioned in many of the 
business publications and science/technology jourals. The internet is 
having a significant implication on our society, and the strict network 
use policy enforced by Scarborough College is disappointing.
 
I should emphasis that I am not in anyway a left-wing thinker. I support 
the value of free-entreprise. I support private entreprises in 
establishing services such as Io.Org and I recognize their work of making 
the network more accessible to the general public. However, the 
university has a different mission than the private sector. I believe 
that a equitable computer use policy is warranted.
 
I support policies that make network services available under a 
restriction. For example, max of 5 ftp sessions concurrently at any 
time. However, I cannot accept a policy that discourages the use of 
'extended service' by commercializing university computer centres.
 
Tom Peng (a324peng@cdf.utoronto.ca)

